  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hortiboletus bubalinus, commonly known as the Ascot Hat, is a bolete fungus recognizable by its distinctive bulbous, rounded cap that resembles a formal ascot hat or button mushroom. This edible species belongs to the bolete family and is found across New England during summer and fall months.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Distinctive Cap Shape",
      "description": "The cap is characteristically rounded and bulbous, often remaining ball-like even at maturity, resembling a button mushroom or formal ascot tie knot",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Pore Surface Color",
      "description": "The yellow to olive-yellow pores beneath the cap are fine and may bruise slightly greenish-brown when handled",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Stem Characteristics",
      "description": "The stem is relatively short and thick with a pale yellow to tan color, often with faint reticulation near the apex",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hortiboletus bubalinus typically fruitsunder hardwood and mixed forests, often near oaks and maples, in rich soil during warm, moist summer weather.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The common name 'Ascot Hat' perfectly describes this mushroom's appearance, as its persistently rounded cap resembles the formal knot of an ascot necktie worn at British horse races.",
